To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current page, you can follow these steps:

### 1. Create the Extension Structure

First, create a new directory for your extension and set up the basic structure:

```bash
mkdir my-page-saver
cd my-page-saver
touch manifest.json
touch content_script.js
touch popup.html
touch popup.js
```

### 2. Define the Manifest File (`manifest.json`)

The `manifest.json` file is the main configuration file for your extension. It specifies the permissions, background scripts, and other metadata.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Page Saver",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the contents of the current page.",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content_script.js"]
    }
  ],
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  }
}
```

### 3. Add Content Script (`content_script.js`)

The content script will run on every page and capture the page's content.

